86508888 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508760 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508888 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d2612f85-d0e6-5136-9f42-955b4fa6d7df" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508891 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508891 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508891 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508612 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508891 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508891 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508891 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508891 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508891 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508891 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d64e9272-ea26-5829-b8f3-83104e2c4323" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508893 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508893 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508893 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508893 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508711 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508893 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d8ff7838-e070-56e4-b546-4f88fee98842" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508898 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508898 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508898 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508898 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508898 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e8fd8fc6-4e2b-5b7a-b070-aeac64a96f45" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508899 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186508797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508899 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508899 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508899 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186508797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508899 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eae4d3f6-b44e-59db-a081-02bc4a4fa2c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508915 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508871 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508915 #edb/ref 1069 "q-347235" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508915 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508915 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ab/ab0416aeff61e4916acc1765a7931da0a41a64ba9d0ac4c05a322de6a1ed431b.png)\n\nA box whose depth $z$ is equal to half of its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$4$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $36\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508915 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "180b48d4-1e2c-527c-b442-f02b5ba67824" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508915 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =4\\cdot 2xz+1\\cdot 2xy+1\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =8xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $36\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 36 & =xz(2z) \\\\ 36 & =2x{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x{z}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =8xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =8\\,(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}})\\,z+2\\,(\\frac{18}{{z}^{2}})\\,(2z)+2(2z)z \\\\  & =\\frac{144}{z}+\\frac{72}{z}+4{z}^{2} \\\\  & =4{z}^{2}+\\frac{216}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 8z-\\frac{216}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 8{z}^{3} & =216 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=8+\\frac{432}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=8+16=24>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508916 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508888 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508916 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345710" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508916 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508916 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9d/9d3a3f70571b0bfca1d4c5251113b915a8061955cb6f48c310d1939571c14e47.png)\n\nConsider a box with a surface area of $432$ square units whose depth $z$ is twice its height $y.$ Find the value of $y$ that maximizes the volume of the box. Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508916 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1a6b4914-aa75-5cc3-80a2-4dde99e3c7aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508916 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot y\\cdot z=x\\cdot y\\cdot (2y)=2x{y}^{2}\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $432$ square units. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xy+2xz+2yz \\\\ 432 & =2xy+2xz+2yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=2y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}432 & =2xy+2x(2y)+2y(2y) \\\\ 432 & =2xy+4xy+4{y}^{2} \\\\ 432 & =6xy+4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{72}{y}-\\frac{2}{3}y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into the expression $V=2x{y}^{2}$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(y) & =2{y}^{2}(\\frac{72}{y}-\\frac{2}{3}y) \\\\  & =144y-\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=144-4{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 144-4{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =36 \\\\ y & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $y$ must be positive.\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=6$ gives a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=-8y\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(6)=-48<0,\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(6)$ is negative, we conclude that this is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has height $y=6$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508919 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508919 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18398" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508919 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508919 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b0/b024db5e134e25212b44cef850118a6823148b01f782c67dccc42f12b82e3854.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $8\\,cm\\times 6\\,cm.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{cm}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume. Round your answer to two decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508919 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "35d71232-f38b-500b-9a6f-4af0bdca3a38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508919 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=8-2y,\\,z=6-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $8-2y>0$ and $6-2y>0$, hence $y<3.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(8-2y)(y)(6-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-28{y}^{2}+48y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-56y+48,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-56y+48 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-14y+12 & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{7\\pm \\sqrt{13}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $\\frac{7+\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ since we already know that $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-56.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{7-\\sqrt{13}}{3}≈1.13$ gives the maximum possible volume." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508922 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508922 #edb/ref 1069 "q-347239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508922 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508922 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/61/61e7943162b7df06af1d6ba805630268e872031e1cb27b566810ee7fc22e6bbc.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $18\\,ft\\times 18\\,ft.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{ft}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height $y$ of the box that gives the maximum possible volume.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508922 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3a8692c9-b01b-529e-b66c-5ef584b340c0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508922 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=18-2y,\\,z=18-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $18-2y>0$ and $18-2y>0$, hence $y<9.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<9.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(18-2y)(y)(18-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-72{y}^{2}+324y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-144y+324,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-144y+324 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-12y+27 & =0 \\\\ y & =3,\\,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $9$ since we already know that $0<y<9.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $3$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-144.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(3)$ is negative, we conclude that $3$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the height of the box with the maximum volume is $y=3\\text{ ft}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508925 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508925 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117067" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508925 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508925 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bf/bf3beb1908abd2d626d4b49efb010f3ac5d7cb82fec7e22abd13fbc457c7202d.png)\n\nConsider a box with a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The volume of the box is $729$ cubic units. Given that the surface area of the box has been minimized, find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508925 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "445bb805-c2f6-5166-9dd9-5663cfa2ac32" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508925 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2{x}^{2}+4xy\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $729$ cubic units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 729 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =729 \\\\ y & =\\frac{729}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2{x}^{2}+4xy \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+4x\\cdot \\frac{729}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+\\frac{2\\,916}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=4x-\\frac{2\\,916}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 4x-\\frac{2\\,916}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{2\\,916}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 4{x}^{3} & =2\\,916 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =729 \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=9$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=4+\\frac{5\\,832}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(9)=4+8=12>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(9)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=9$ is indeed a minimum of $S(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the smallest possible surface area has side length $x=9$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508934 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508934 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81624" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508934 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508934 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5c/5cd38fb898d5cc3fcef43ae6397b02f22fbd06cbe598263fe77ce6b936c16bae.png)\n\nA box has a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The surface area of the box is $216$ square units. Given that the volume of the box has been maximized, find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508934 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c2829d5-9e3f-5d96-8caf-fe7beef5d605" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508934 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such as the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot x\\cdot y={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $216$ square units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\,\\text{(area of the base) }+4\\,\\text{(area of  one side) } \\\\ 216 & =2{x}^{2}+4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation: \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{54}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{54}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}$ in the expression $V={x}^{2}y,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(x) & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{54}{x}-\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =54x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(x)=54-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 54-\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =36 \\\\ x & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step we only considered the positive root because the side length $x$ must be positive. \n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=6$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(x)=-3x\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(6)=-18<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(6)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=6$ is indeed a maximum of $V(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has side length $x=6$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508935 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508935 #edb/ref 1069 "q-315628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508935 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508935 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/85/8548c87f15edc7e7c903c753767f12bd6f4c6f0294ff07734659583907f2ea4a.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $2$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508935 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f708375-cdc1-53f8-8bb4-b17c1643502c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508935 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $180\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 180 & =2{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{z}^{2}y & =180 \\\\ y & =\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\  & =10(2z)z+4(2z)(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}})+4\\,(\\frac{90}{{z}^{2}})\\,z \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{720}{z}+\\frac{360}{z} \\\\  & =20{z}^{2}+\\frac{1080}{z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 40z-\\frac{1080}{{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 40{z}^{3} & =1080 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =27 \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=3$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=40+\\frac{2160}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(3)=40+80=120>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(3)$ is positive, we conclude that $z=3$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $z=3$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508937 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508937 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346825" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508937 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508937 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c6/c652b4ce5aa631211824e1f05160bf870c29d3c4d54ea54c29c627aaf7754eea.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is four times its height. Given that the box's volume is $800$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508937 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "64a50494-969c-52da-a2bf-9af25982f51d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508937 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4x{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $x$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(4y)+2xy+2y(4y) \\\\  & =8xy+2xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10xy+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =10(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}})y+8{y}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2000}{y}+8{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2000}{5} \\\\  & =600\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508943 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508943 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86444" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508943 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508943 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/c4/c4bbdc87980efd87c64fdb8fa693078817e16bbff136f94a2ac4dfc2c651b17d.png)\n\nConsider a box with a surface area of $48$ square units whose width $x$ is twice its depth $z.$ Given that the volume of the box has been maximized, find the value of $z.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508943 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "761b830c-0a32-54fa-8bad-182a530f7511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508943 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such as the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot y\\cdot z=2z\\cdot y\\cdot z=2{z}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $48$ square units. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xy+2xz+2yz \\\\ 48 & =2xy+2xz+2yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=2z,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =2(2z)y+2(2z)z+2yz \\\\ 48 & =4yz+4{z}^{2}+2yz \\\\ 48 & =6zy+4{z}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{8}{z}-\\frac{2z}{3}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into the expression $V=2{z}^{2}y,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(z) & =2{z}^{2}(\\frac{8}{z}-\\frac{2z}{3}) \\\\  & =16z-\\frac{4{z}^{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(z)=16-4{z}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 16-4{z}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4{z}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =4 \\\\ z & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step we only considered the positive root because the side length $z$ must be positive. \n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=2$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(z)=-8z\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(2)=-16<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(2)$ is negative, we conclude that $z=2$ is indeed a maximum of $V(z).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has the depth $z=2$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508952 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508854 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508952 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346764" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508952 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508952 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/1e/1e039fda5af235c4a9948853295846eaadb064fede888b84fdb5be552a35e3f1.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is one-third its width. Given that the box's volume is $288$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508952 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90ab0435-552f-51ca-b675-8d58a2c58837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508952 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $288$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 288 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{3},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n288=xy(\\frac{x}{3})=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{3} & =288 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =864 \\\\ y & =\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(\\frac{x}{3})+2x(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{864}{{x}^{2}})(\\frac{x}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1728}{x}+\\frac{576}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{2}+\\frac{2304}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x-\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}x & =\\frac{2304}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{3}{x}^{3} & =2304 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =1728 \\\\ x & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{4608}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(12)=\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}=4>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(12)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=12$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(12) & =\\frac{2}{3}(12)^{2}+\\frac{2304}{12} \\\\  & =96+192 \\\\  & =288\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508958 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508958 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117209" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508958 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508958 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d7/d77c003f88d428578c7f249feb4d0d086c8151c1ef21348d806a0baba751000d.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The width of the box is four times its height. Given that the box's volume is $800$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area of the box?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508958 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "97252151-ba80-5f2a-b51f-786d26797ca5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508958 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is exactly $800$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 800 & =xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=4y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n800=4z{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $z$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z{y}^{2} & =800 \\\\ z & =\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2(4y)(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}})+2(4y)y+2y(\\frac{200}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,600}{y}+8{y}^{2}+\\frac{400}{y} \\\\  & =8{y}^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(y)=16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 16y-\\frac{2\\,000}{{y}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3}-2\\,000 & =0 \\\\ 16{y}^{3} & =2\\,000 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =125 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=5$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(y)=16+\\frac{4\\,000}{{y}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(5)=16+32=48>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(5)$ is positive, we conclude that $y=5$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(5) & =8(5)^{2}+\\frac{2\\,000}{5}=600.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508960 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8c/8cb99cea56d8a57b607589305bfe225610c63504239296e0b6eb81ba220feec8.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $6\\,in\\times 6\\,in.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{in}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d40af61-e6e1-509f-9bd7-a155591f2abe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508960 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the side length $x$  of the tray is given by\n\n$$\nx=6-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have that $6-2y>0$ hence $y<3.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =(6-2y)^{2}y \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-24{y}^{2}+36y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-48y+36,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-48y+36 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-4y+3 & =0 \\\\ (y-3)(y-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =3,\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $3$ since we already know that $0<y<3.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $1$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-48.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(1)=-24$ is negative, we conclude that $1$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=1$ gives the maximum possible volume." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508970 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508822 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508970 #edb/ref 1069 "q-315610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508970 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508970 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d8/d85a061ab65d269f22f589e7f57b07a6d474db5f6f29c8c8b412701b99de5241.png)\n\nA box has a square base with side lengths $x$ and height $y,$ as shown above. The surface area of the box is $294$ square units. Find the value of $x$ that maximizes the volume of the box. Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508970 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b92c6e01-0c5a-5f6e-a138-545af5c6ad5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508970 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $294$ square units. So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\,\\text{(area of the base)}+4\\,\\text{(area of one side)} \\\\ 294 & =2{x}^{2}+4xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{147}{2x}-\\frac{x}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $y=\\frac{147}{2x}-\\frac{x}{2}$ in the expression $V={x}^{2}y,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(x) & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{147}{2x}-\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{147}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(x)=\\frac{147}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{147}{2}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =49 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $x$ must be positive. \n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=7$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(x)=-3x\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(7)=-21<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(7)$ is negative, we conclude that $x=7$ is indeed a maximum of $V(x).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has side length $x=7$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508974 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508852 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508974 #edb/ref 1069 "q-345713" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508974 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508974 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/18/1859fcfa509ac8b8c93feed744df6fb66e87fc318811d75ad3cd154c6ab1bb96.png)\n\nConsider a box with a surface area of $196$ square units whose height $y$ is three halves its width $x.$ Find the value of $y$ that maximizes the volume of the box. Do not round the answer.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508974 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c4e37c29-e44f-56ff-9916-25bc220edbdb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508974 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=x\\cdot y\\cdot z\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\nSince $y=\\frac{3}{2}x,$ we have $x=\\frac{2}{3}y,$ so\n\n$$\nV=(\\frac{2}{3}y)\\cdot y\\cdot z=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}z.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The surface area is $196$ square units. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xy+2xz+2yz \\\\ 196 & =2xy+2xz+2yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $x=\\frac{2}{3}y,$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}196 & =2(\\frac{2}{3}y)y+2(\\frac{2}{3}y)z+2yz \\\\ 196 & =\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{4}{3}yz+2yz \\\\ 196 & =\\frac{4}{3}{y}^{2}+\\frac{10}{3}yz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make one of the variables the subject in the constraint equation:\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{294}{5y}-\\frac{2}{5}y\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into the expression $V=\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}z$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(y) & =\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{2}(\\frac{294}{5y}-\\frac{2}{5}y) \\\\  & =\\frac{196}{5}y-\\frac{4}{15}{y}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=\\frac{196}{5}-\\frac{4}{5}{y}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{196}{5}-\\frac{4}{5}{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 196-4{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =49 \\\\ y & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that in the last step, we only considered the positive root because the side length $y$ must be positive.\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $y=7$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=-\\frac{8}{5}y\\,⟹\\,{V}^{″}(7)=-\\frac{56}{5}<0,\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(7)$ is negative, we conclude that $y=7$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the box with the largest possible volume has height $y=7$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508978 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508893 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508978 #edb/ref 1069 "q-315627" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508978 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508978 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/a9/a9de332f316c91f9739208d11828f6a0f7d035589dc4f3adc921e5108453cc12.png)\n\nA box with no lid and a square base has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$5$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $10\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}} units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508978 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "caab3419-40f4-5f42-9fc6-7d0c53c70e6a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508978 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 4xy \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}+8xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $10\\,{m}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot x\\cdot y \\\\ 10 & ={x}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}y & =10 \\\\ y & =\\frac{10}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5{x}^{2}+8xy \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}+8x\\cdot \\frac{10}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =5{x}^{2}+\\frac{80}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(x)=10x-\\frac{80}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 10x-\\frac{80}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 10{x}^{3} & =80 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =8 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=2$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(x)=10+\\frac{160}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(2)=10+20=30>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(2)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a minimum of $C(x).$\n\nTherefore, the production cost is the smallest when $x=2$ units." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508844 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508981 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cf/cf7c07babafa92e2c88e2e08727edc0d724b05f1318f6cf022d9f8b31ff668d7.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $15\\,in\\times 8\\,in.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{in}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d416390a-8b08-58ae-b4bd-805af1c30ba3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508981 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=15-2y,\\,z=8-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $15-2y>0$ and $8-2y>0$, hence $y<4.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(15-2y)(y)(8-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-46{y}^{2}+120y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-92y+120,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-92y+120 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-23y+30 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-6) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $6$ since we already know that $0<y<4.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-92.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{5}{3})$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508985 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508985 #edb/ref 1069 "q-3478" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508985 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508985 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/31/31df5fe79f2aa8715321b6500474683a01732788e5fafc3300dfffc83c266893.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $3$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508985 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9422237-5095-57b7-8cd1-109d7184ed3e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508985 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $z$ such that the manufacturing cost\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =5\\cdot 2xz+2\\cdot 2xy+2\\cdot 2yz \\\\  & =10xz+4xy+4yz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume must be $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$ So, we have the constraint equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot z\\cdot y \\\\ 50 & =3{z}^{2}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{2}y & =50 \\\\ y & =\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $C,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =10xz+4xy+4yz \\\\ C & =10(3z)z+4(3z)(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}})+4(\\frac{50}{3{z}^{2}})z \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{200}{z}+\\frac{200}{3z} \\\\ C & =30{z}^{2}+\\frac{800}{3z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${C}^{′}(z)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{C}^{′}(z)=60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}^{′}(z) & =0 \\\\ 60z-\\frac{800}{3{z}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 180{z}^{3} & =800 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =\\frac{800}{180} \\\\ z & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{C}^{″}(z)=60+\\frac{1\\,600}{{z}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${C}^{″}(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}})$ is positive, we conclude that $z=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{40}{9}}$ is indeed a minimum of $C(z).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346760" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508988 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6f/6f753b0f958204f705f0d59610210ae20e8f3096f0a9847bf72e2d3ab43a6305.png)\n\nConsider a box with width $x,$ height $y,$ and depth $z,$ as shown above. The depth of the box is half its width. Given that the box's volume is $243$ cubic units, what is the smallest possible surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ of the box? Do not round the answer.\n\n${S}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}} square units" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e2adf7b9-c049-5411-a710-9c822b654299" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508988 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the value of $x$ such that the surface area\n\n$$\nS=2xz+2xy+2yz\n$$\n\nis the smallest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: The volume is $243$ cubic units. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =x\\cdot y\\cdot z \\\\ 243 & =xyz.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $z=\\frac{x}{2},$ we get the constraint equation\n\n$$\n243=xy(\\frac{x}{2})=\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2}.\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We make $y$ the subject of the constraint equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{2} & =243 \\\\ {x}^{2}y & =486 \\\\ y & =\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute the above into our expression for $S,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2xz+2xy+2yz \\\\  & =2x(\\frac{x}{2})+2x(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}})+2(\\frac{486}{{x}^{2}})(\\frac{x}{2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{972}{x}+\\frac{486}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${S}^{′}(x)=0.$ Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{S}^{′}(x)=2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 2x-\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{1458}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 2{x}^{3} & =1458 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =729 \\\\ x & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $x=9$ is a minimum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{S}^{″}(x)=2+\\frac{2916}{{x}^{3}}\\,⟹\\,{S}^{″}(9)=2+4=6>0.\n$$\n\nSince ${S}^{″}(9)$ is positive, we conclude that $x=9$ is indeed a minimum.\n\nNow, the smallest surface area ${S}_{\\text{min}}$ that the box can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\text{min}}=S(9) & ={9}^{2}+\\frac{1458}{9} \\\\  & =243\\,\\text{square units}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508998 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186508891 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508998 #edb/ref 1069 "q-117300" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508998 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508998 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/dd/dd5731dcea0cc6bc8ffc0aad963f4b05b57a7422512a2df3df64666e63254f77.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $10\\,in\\times 10\\,in.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{in}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height of the box that gives the maximum possible volume." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508998 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcc5cb30-3a54-5c1c-aecb-10ee16fa7222" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186508998 #edb/ref 1074 "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x$ and $y$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV={x}^{2}y\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the side length $x$ of the tray is given by\n\n$$\nx=10-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0,$ we must have that $10-2y>0$ hence $y<5.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={x}^{2}y \\\\  & =(10-2y)^{2}y \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-40{y}^{2}+100y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-80y+100,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-80y+100 & =0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-20y+25 & =0 \\\\ (3y-5)(y-5) & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{3},\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $5$ since we already know that $0<y<5.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-80.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(\\frac{5}{3})=-40$ is negative, we conclude that $\\frac{5}{3}$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the stationary point $y=\\frac{5}{3}$ gives the maximum possible volume." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
